ep 15 frames (paused), read nametable heart tiles. If changed → snapshot f2, diff decreased; break. Else continue.\n\nBut each loop iteration is 2 calls; a hit might take ~100-300 frames = ~7-20 iterations. Acceptable? Maybe 40 calls. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, step 30 frames per iteration → fewer iterations (~10). And the diff window is 30 frames → ~10-20 changes.\n\nActually, even better: I don't need to detect the hit via nametable. I can just take a screenshot after each 30-frame burst; but reading nametable is cheaper.\n\nHmm, let me simplify: stand still; take bursts of 60 frames; screenshot; when hearts change, snapshot and diff. The diff covers up to 60 frames of enemy movement. Earlier d_h2 over 240 frames had only 18 decreased — quite small. So a 60-frame window should give <18. Health should be among them... but it wasn't for 240! So health doesn't decrease.\n\nTHEREFORE health must be stored as something that does NOT decrease when damaged. Possibilities: inverted damage counter that increases. But d_h2 increased had 17 and no small counter.\n\nHmm, unless the health is stored as a MULTI-byte value, or in a packed nibble, or the value changed by a non-obvious amount.\n\nWait wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the \"health\" is displayed via OAM sprites, and the RAM stores the number of hearts as a small number but the game updates it via a routine that... it must be in RAM.\n\nLet me just carefully look at a full RAM dump vs known. Let me read 0x0000-0x00FF now (death screen) and compare with what I expect.\n\nHmm, I'm at the death screen. Let me press CONTINUE to get full health, read 0x0000-0x00FF and 0x0600-0x06FF, then take a hit and re-read.\n\nActually, here's a cleaner method to get exactly one hit: **Use the Octorok's rock**. Not deterministic.\n\nAlternatively, use a **damaging tile**: In Zelda, some overworld screens have \"water\"? no.\n\nHmm, what about the \"spike\" enemies (like the \"Blade Trap\")? Not here.\n\nOK, let me just do the step-and-read